  "body": "Currently `<OPTGROUP>` is only allowed as a direct child of `<SELECT>`.\r\n\r\nThe proposal is to also allow `<OPTGROUP>` to be a parent of `<OPTGROUP>` in the HTML standard, thus allowing a semantically accurate and mobile friendly hierarchy of options - nested option groups.\r\n\r\nThis was recommended on the HTML4 reference:\r\nhttps://www.w3.org/TR/html401/interact/forms.html#h-17.6\r\n\r\n> _Note. Implementors are advised that future versions of HTML may extend the grouping mechanism to allow for nested groups (i.e., OPTGROUP elements may nest). This will allow authors to represent a richer hierarchy of choices._\r\n\r\nThe common workarounds today are:\r\n\r\n1. Adding (em)spaces to the start of the option element. This causes visual bugs on mobile, RTL issues, and it disables the usability of jumping to an option by pressing its first letter.\r\n2. Adding padding. Not supported in mobile. Not supported in some browsers. \r\n\r\nHere is a usage example:\r\nhttps://bug79431.bmoattachments.org/attachment.cgi?id=37115\r\n\r\np.s. this proposal is far from being original or new. i found a mention of this in 1997, which was rejected because the developers of Netscape were overwhelmed at the time...\r\n\r\nrelated to:\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#the-optgroup-element",
